## Rules

Add a rule when the project learns one, with the date and the reason.

- **Originals are immutable.** Cleaning reads `data/original/` and writes elsewhere. A changed upstream file is refetched with `--fresh`, which keeps the old hash in the manifest.
- **Probe before parsing.** Open real files from every era of the source before writing or changing a parser, and record what you found in the decision-log.
- **One source until there are two.** The registry and concept catalog come with the second source, following the skill's "Adding a second source" procedure.
- **Failures are loud.** A failed artifact is in `data/audit/extraction_errors.json` and the audit summary; rejected rows are in `data/audit/rejected.csv` with a reason. Never hide a failure, and never fill a gap with sample, synthetic or stale data.
- **Numbers in prose are generated.** Don't type counts into the README or this file; `audit` fills the generated blocks and a test fails when they are stale.
- **Schema changes are contract changes.** Changing `LONG_COLUMNS`, a type or the natural key needs a `CHANGELOG.md` entry saying what breaks for users.
- **Storage budget.** Committed, Git LFS or external storage per source, as recorded in the decision-log; CI fails on any file near 100 MB.
<!-- IF:PUBLISH -->
- **Publish only what visibility allows.** Datasette, the Quarto site and DocumentCloud receive only what the visibility and sensitivity decisions in the decision-log permit. Corrections arrive through the *Data correction* issue form and are logged in `CHANGELOG.md`.
<!-- /IF -->
<!-- IF:REFRESH -->
- **Refreshes arrive as pull requests.** The scheduled workflow never commits to `main`; review its audit diff and spot-check sample before merging. A scheduled run can be late or skipped, so check `data/audit/discovery.md` for gaps.
<!-- /IF -->
<!-- IF:MULTI_SOURCE -->
- **Concepts carry caveats.** Every concept in `data/lookups/concepts.yaml` that maps more than one source says what is and is not comparable; a test enforces it.
<!-- /IF -->

## Pull requests

A pull request says what changed, why, what you checked (tests, the audit summary diff, reconcile), and what you left alone on purpose. A PR that changes data carries the audit diff; a new reconcile mismatch blocks the merge until it is explained.

